<section class="section">
	<div class="wrap">
		<p class="runhead"><span>Sub-Sub</span><span class="mid">Students and privacy</span></p>
	</div>
	<div class="wrap pair">
		<div>
			<h2>It helps you learn to read the literature.</h2>
			<div class="prose-block">
				<p>Type <code>/read</code> and a citekey, DOI or PMID to read a paper with guidance. In the browser, select Read a paper, or Read next to a result of the library search. Sub-Sub finds the passages and asks questions; you read them and write the answers. For a literature review, type <code>/lit reading list:</code> and your topic. You get the works to read and questions to read them with, and no results.</p>
				<p>Sub-Sub does not draft text for your assignments, even when you ask. For a thesis, it drafts text only when you ask, and marks it. The rules of your course about AI still apply.</p>
				<p>You can try it for free with a Google key, and an empty Zotero library is fine.</p>
				<p><a href="/students/">For students</a> · <a href="/pt/estudantes/" lang="pt-PT" hreflang="pt-PT">Para estudantes</a></p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div>
			<h2>Your library stays in Zotero.</h2>
			<div class="prose-block">
				<p>Sub-Sub talks to Zotero through its local API and never uses your zotero.org account. It sends no usage data.</p>
				<p>The model provider that you choose receives your messages and what the tools return: titles, authors, abstracts, and parts of a full text when you ask for a note or a guided reading. PubMed, Europe PMC, OpenAlex, Crossref and Unpaywall receive search terms and identifiers, not your notes.</p>
				<p><a href="/privacy/">What goes where</a></p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

